  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Friday)\nIssued at 1145 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nSoutherly winds gusting 20 to 30 mph and above seasonal average\ntemperatures continue again during the day Saturday ahead of the\nlow pressure system forecast to reach the Oklahoma Texas Panhandles\nSaturday evening. Limited fire weather concerns and locally \nenhanced grassland fire spread rates also are forecast Saturday \nafternoon. \n\nAs the low pressure system moves out into the southern/central\nPlains Saturday evening, surface low and an associated cold front\nset up from the Panhandles into Kansas and move east/southeast\nwith the parent mid/upper trof. Ahead of these features, a deeper\nplume of moisture is progged to lift northeast into the region\nwithin an increasing 30-40KT low level jet. A marginal elevated \ninstability axis is also forecast to setup along the moisture/low\nlevel jet axis. Thus, shower and thunderstorm chances develop \nwithin these conditions out west and should lift northeast and \npotentially move into parts of northeast Oklahoma Saturday \nevening. Through the evening hours, elevated instability looks to \nweaken with eastward extent, and as such will carry low thunder \nprobs mainly along and west of Interstate 44 becoming more \nisolated for the rest of northeast Oklahoma. \n\nShower/storm chances Saturday evening lift northeast overnight\nwith the low level jet, while additional rain showers spread into\nthe CWA with the cold front Saturday night and Sunday morning. \nLatest model solutions have slowed the progression of the front \nwith it now entering northeast Oklahoma closer to 09z give or take\nan hour, and exiting southeast Oklahoma/northwest Arkansas early \nafternoon Sunday. In response, have slowed the timing of rain \nchances tapering off from west to east with PoPs lingering in \nnorthwest Arkansas early Sunday afternoon. Thunder potentials with\nthe front remain generally below 15 percent for the morning \nhours, and then begin to increase for the afternoon. If showers \nhold on longer into the afternoon, a slight chance of thunder \ncould develop for northwest Arkansas. \n\nBehind the frontal passage, gusty northerly winds transporting a\ncolder airmass will spread over the CWA with temperatures by late\nafternoon Sunday in the upper 40s to near 60 deg from north to \nsouth. Winds should subside Sunday night with cloud cover becoming\nmore scattered as the mid/upper level trof axis exits off to the\neast. The colder airmass sets up over the region allowing low\ntemperatures Monday morning near/slightly below freezing for the \nnorthern half of the CWA. Another cold night is forecast Monday \nnight/Tuesday morning with lows in the 20s to mid 30s for the CWA.\n\nTuesday into the latter half of next week, model solutions\ncontinue to indicate a shortwave moving across the southern Plains\nTuesday/Wednesday though differ on details. Latest trends continue\nto shift the track a little more southward, though temperature\nprofiles are not quite as cold as 24-hrs ago. Moisture continues \nto look limited for this wave and will stay with persistence of \nPoPs below mentionable criteria Tuesday night/Wednesday. Depending\non how this evolves, isolated drizzle/light rain could \npotentially develop near the upper low center in southeast \nOklahoma. In the wake of this shortwave, there is potential for\nadditional waves to track into/across the southern Plains late\nweek into next weekend. Will continue to monitor for precip\npotentials. Either way though, the cooler airmass look to stay for\nnext week with highs in the 40s/50s and lows in the 20s/30s.",
